---
import BaseLayout from "../../../../shared/components/BaseLayout.astro";
import TypeBadge from "../../../../shared/components/TypeBadge.astro";
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { MAIN_SITE_URL, DATA_SITE_URL } from "../../../../shared/config/config";
const n = (await getCollection('experiences')).filter((e) => e.data.privacy_status !== "synthetic").length;
const articleJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "What the Accounts Use",
  description: "A strategy map from the recovery accounts with all the caveats, and why relapse rates are not computed here.",
  url: `${MAIN_SITE_URL}/analysis/strategies/`,
  dateModified: "2026-10-05",
  author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "SelfControlAtlas", url: MAIN_SITE_URL },
  publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "SelfControlAtlas", url: MAIN_SITE_URL },
  about: ["problematic pornography use", "recovery", "community data analysis"]
};
---
<BaseLayout
  siteTitle="Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform"
  siteUrl={MAIN_SITE_URL}
  title="What the Accounts Use: A Strategy Map With All the Caveats"
  description="Editorial analysis of strategy mentions across community accounts — what dominates, what clusters, and why no relapse rate can be computed here."
  canonical="/analysis/strategies/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Analysis", url: "/analysis/" }, { name: "Strategies", url: "/analysis/strategies/" }]}
  jsonLd={articleJsonLd}
>
  <h1>What the Accounts Use: A Strategy Map With All the Caveats</h1>
  <TypeBadge type="pattern" />
  <p class="lede">Across the {n} processed accounts, strategy mentions form a steep pyramid:
  one strategy dominates, a handful cluster beneath it, and everything else scatters. This essay
  maps the pyramid — and explains why the obvious-sounding conclusions from it must not be drawn.
  Analysis, not science; opinions are labeled as mine.</p>

  <h2>The pyramid</h2>
  <p><strong>Structured routine (62 mentions)</strong> stands alone at the top. Beneath it:
  exercise (14), sleep hygiene (12), social accountability (11), journaling (6), meditation (6).
  Everything else — blockers, media reduction, phone restrictions, environment change — appears
  a handful of times each.</p>
  <p>My first opinion: <strong>this pyramid is not a ranking of effectiveness. It is a ranking of
  how the community talks.</strong> Routine is mentioned by everyone because routine is the
  community's grammar — its default answer, its first advice, its closing line. That 62 people
  mention it tells you what they believe, not what works. The two questions are different.</p>

  <h2>The temptation, declined</h2>
  <p>It is tempting to compute relapse rates per strategy and rank them. I computed them, and I
  am not publishing them as findings, because the numbers are meaningless: tiny samples,
  self-selected accounts, mentions rather than trials. What the honest version shows is only
  this: <strong>strategy mentions do not separate the relapsed from the clean in this dataset
  in any way worth trusting.</strong> Sleep hygiene and meditation show zero relapses among
  their handful of users; social accountability shows a higher share. These are noise-level
  differences. Any claim built on them would be false precision wearing a table.</p>

  <h2>What the long-term accounts change about the picture</h2>
  <p>There is one comparison with a little more signal: the thirteen one-year-plus accounts.
  Twelve mention structured routine, and beyond it they scatter — one blocker, one media
  reduction, one phone restriction, one exercise. My second opinion: <strong>the long-term
  accounts suggest not that routine works, but that anything works if it is embedded in a
  routine.</strong> The tool varied. The container did not. That is an observation about the
  accounts, not evidence about outcomes.</p>

  <h2>How to use a strategy map honestly</h2>
  <p>A strategy map built from community reports is useful for exactly three things: learning
  what people actually try, finding practices you had not considered, and calibrating
  expectations about what the community emphasizes. It is not useful for choosing your strategy
  by popularity, and it cannot rank strategies by effectiveness. For that, the platform's
  evidence layer — where each strategy's research support is rated separately — is the right
  place to look.</p>

  <p><a href="/strategies/">Browse all strategies</a> ·
  <a href={DATA_SITE_URL + "/evidence/"}>Evidence standards</a> ·
  <a href="/analysis/">Main analysis</a> ·
  <a href="/statistics/">Live statistics</a></p>
</BaseLayout>
